<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>

  <!-- ===========================================================================================
       Moves the vanilla shelves and bookcases into UnifiedStorage.

       <b>Why every previous attempt at this failed.</b> None of these four defs has a
       designationCategory element of its own. It is declared exactly once in the whole chain, on
       FurnitureBase, as "Furniture":

           FurnitureBase   designationCategory: Furniture
             ShelfBase
               StorageShelfBase       Shelf, ShelfSmall
               BookcaseBase           Bookcase, BookcaseSmall

       So the obvious patch, a PatchOperationReplace on
       Defs/ThingDef[defName="Bookcase"]/designationCategory, selects nothing at all. RimWorld reports the
       operation as failed and the bookcase does not move. Nothing is wrong with the xpath except that the
       element it names does not exist on that def: the value the game ends up using is inherited, and
       inheritance is resolved after patching.

       <b>Add, not Replace.</b> An element added to the child overrides what it would have inherited, so
       adding designationCategory to the concrete def is what moves it. The conditional Remove before it
       exists only to keep this correct if a future version of the game starts declaring the element on
       these defs: adding a second one would leave the def carrying two.

       <b>Why the four defs are listed rather than patching ShelfBase.</b> Patching the shared parent is
       tempting and would catch modded shelves for free, but it reaches further than it looks. Everything
       else in the game and in mods that derives from ShelfBase declares its own category and so is
       unaffected, with two exceptions that inherit: an abstract weapon rack base, and a food spot from
       another mod. A food spot is not storage, and quietly moving another mod's content is not this
       patch's business. Modded shelves are handled by Architect_StorageSweep.xml instead, where the test
       is what the category calls itself rather than what a def happens to inherit from.

       Shelf and ShelfSmall are included alongside the two bookcases. A Storage tab that did not contain
       the vanilla shelf would be an odd thing to have built.
       =========================================================================================== -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <success>Normal</success>
    <operations>

      <li Class="PatchOperationConditional">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="Shelf" or defName="ShelfSmall" or defName="Bookcase" or defName="BookcaseSmall"]/designationCategory</xpath>
        <match Class="PatchOperationRemove">
          <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="Shelf" or defName="ShelfSmall" or defName="Bookcase" or defName="BookcaseSmall"]/designationCategory</xpath>
        </match>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <xpath>Defs/ThingDef[defName="Shelf" or defName="ShelfSmall" or defName="Bookcase" or defName="BookcaseSmall"]</xpath>
        <value>
          <designationCategory>UnifiedStorage</designationCategory>
        </value>
      </li>

    </operations>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
